================================================================================
METHOD — STANDING-ORDERS §7 (scapy, not ping)
================================================================================
A ping proves a path exists; it cannot show a relay rewriting giaddr. Each leg sends 5 distinct
crafted DHCPDISCOVERs (unique chaddr and xid per client, BOOTP broadcast flag set so replies
need no ARP of the client) from eth3, and counts THREE independent ways:
  1. tcpdump on eth3  — what we actually put on the wire (not what the tool claims);
  2. tcpdump on eth4  — what the DUT relayed;
  3. the scapy DHCP server's own per-frame record on eth4 — giaddr, hops, source, chaddr.
Each positive leg is paired with a control in the same configuration that must produce ZERO on
eth4. Counts below are from the captures, never from the sending tool's own tally.

================================================================================
STEP 1 — the relay forwards, rewrites giaddr and increments hops (leg `relay-on`)
================================================================================
  dhcp4.py client eth3 --mac 02:d4:00:00:50:01 --clients 5 --gap 1 --broadcast \
          --prl 1,3,6,51 --wait 3
  dhcp4.py server eth4 --server-ip 192.168.48.2 --pool-start 192.168.10.100 \
          --pool-end 192.168.10.110 --mask 255.255.255.0 --router 192.168.10.1 \
          --lease 3600 --echo-option82

  COUNTED:
    eth3 DISCOVERs/REQUESTs we sent  : 10   (5 DISCOVER + 5 REQUEST)
    eth4 frames relayed by the DUT   : 10   (5 DISCOVER + 5 REQUEST)  — 1:1, no loss
    eth3 replies delivered to clients: 10   (5 OFFER + 5 ACK)
    offers received 5/5, acks received 5/5

  The relayed DISCOVERs as the server saw them on eth4 — every field that matters:
    giaddr=192.168.10.1   hops=1  src=192.168.48.1  chaddr=02:d4:00:00:50:01
    giaddr=192.168.10.1   hops=1  src=192.168.48.1  chaddr=02:d4:00:00:50:02
    giaddr=192.168.10.1   hops=1  src=192.168.48.1  chaddr=02:d4:00:00:50:03
    giaddr=192.168.10.1   hops=1  src=192.168.48.1  chaddr=02:d4:00:00:50:04
    giaddr=192.168.10.1   hops=1  src=192.168.48.1  chaddr=02:d4:00:00:50:05
    leased: .100 .101 .102 .103 .104 to the five chaddrs respectively

  - **giaddr rewritten** from 0.0.0.0 to 192.168.10.1, the client-side SVI — which is what lets
    the server pick the client's subnet.
  - **hops incremented** 0 -> 1.
  - Source address 192.168.48.1 (the server-side SVI), unicast to the configured server.
  - Each client's own chaddr preserved, and each got a distinct address.
  - The return path works end to end: every OFFER and ACK reached the right client on eth3.
  Forwarding latency, measured in an isolated capture of both NICs:
    16:04:26.118818 eth3 ... Request from 02:d4:00:00:20:01
    16:04:26.119574 eth4 ... Request from 02:d4:00:00:20:01    -> 0.76 ms
STEP 1 PASS.

