================================================================================
HARNESS FAULTS FOUND AND FIXED (none is a DUT fault)
================================================================================
These cost the first phase of this case. All four were in MY instrumentation, and each one
produced the same misleading symptom — "the relay dropped the packets" — while the relay was
in fact forwarding every frame within a millisecond. Recorded in full because each would
silently corrupt any future relay or DHCP measurement on this bench.

H-1  **tcpdump `-Z $(whoami)` cannot write to the NFS lab home.** Adopted at the start of this
     group to avoid root-owned captures. tcpdump drops privilege before writing, and the lab
     home is root-squashed NFS, so the capture file was created with a pcap header and nothing
     else — a silent empty capture that reads as "no traffic". FIX: write captures to the box's
     LOCAL /tmp as root, then copy and chown. Group A's pcaps were fine precisely because they
     were written as root.

H-2  **Copying a capture while tcpdump is still running yields a truncated/empty file.** The
     leg script waited only on the server process, not on the two tcpdumps, then copied. The
     copied file had no packets. Symptom: "eth3 DISCOVERs we actually sent: 0" for frames that
     certainly went out. FIX: bare `wait` for ALL background jobs before reading any capture.

H-3  **A fresh `dhcp4.py` subprocess per client costs several seconds of scapy import**, so a
     loop intended to send 9 clients 3 s apart actually emitted them ~9 s apart and only the
     first 5 fell inside the capture window. The missing 4 looked exactly like relay loss.
     FIX: `--clients N` sends N distinct clients from ONE process (generic, now in the tool).

H-4  **🔴 The one that matters beyond this bench: a relayed DHCP frame is INVISIBLE to scapy
     by default.** scapy binds BOOTP to UDP only for the exact port pairs (sport 68, dport 67)
     and (sport 67, dport 68). An AW+ relay forwards to the server from an EPHEMERAL source
     port — measured here as `192.168.48.1.57304 > 192.168.48.2.67`. That frame is therefore
     not dissected as BOOTP at all; it reads as Raw, so `BOOTP in pkt` is False and every
     scapy-based server, sniffer or decoder reports **nothing arrived**, while tcpdump on the
     same interface prints the packet plainly. This is precisely the trap that would grade a
     perfectly working relay as broken. FIX, now in dhcp4.py at import:
         bind_layers(UDP, BOOTP, dport=67)
         bind_layers(UDP, BOOTP, dport=68)
     Evidence of the before/after, same bench, same configuration, minutes apart:
         before: eth4 relayed 4 (tcpdump)  |  server RECEIVED 0
         after : eth4 relayed 10 (tcpdump) |  server RECEIVED 5 DISCOVERs with giaddr and hops
     This belongs in `platforms/x960.md` or the tools README as a standing trap; it is in the
     dhcp4.py README entry now.

Also hit, and already a documented repo trap: `pkill -f "tcpdump -i eth"` over ssh matched the
ssh wrapper's own command line and killed the session (exit 255). TESTBOX-ACCESS.md §5 and
memory `ssh-pgrep-watchers-self-match` both warn about it. No `pkill -f` was used afterwards;
background jobs are ended by their own `timeout` and reaped with `wait`.

